    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1865\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to stopping,\nstanding or parking in specified places\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nThe purpose of the bill is to allow residents and homeowners with\nrestrictive driveways, to have the legal authorization to park in\ntheir driveways, but across the sidewalk, only late at night and early\nin the morning, between 11 p.m. and 7 a.m. upon registration with the\nPolice Department.\n\nA new section is added to the VTL, Art. 32, §1202 (b) which would\nallow residents with small, restrictive driveways to park in their\ndriveways and if necessary overhang sidewalks between the hours of\n11:00 p.m. and 7:00 a.m.\n\n THE CURRENT LAW :\nSubsection (a)1.b. of Art. 32, §1202 of the Vehicle and Traffic Law\nentitled, \"Stopping, standing or parking prohibited in specified\nplaces,\" prohibits parking \"On a sidewalk.\"\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection one authorizes residents in the Village of Pelham who live in\nolder homes that have small, restrictive driveways to park in their\ndriveways and overhang sidewalks between the hours of 11:00 p.m. and\n7:00 a.m.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe Village of Pelham is a small village, with 1,903 parcels, about\n3,300 housing units, and approximately 6,500 residents in a village\nthat is approximately eight tenths (.8) square miles.  The age of the\naverage house structure in Pelham is roughly dated in the late 1920's,\nor early 1930's, and driveways and garages for these homes were built\nin an era when passenger cars were much smaller than the vehicles of\ntoday.\n\nThe Village of Pelham for decades has maintained a village-wide\nprohibition on overnight parking on our streets, between the hours of\n2 a.m. and 6 a.m.  for three primary reasons:\n\n1. security/law enforcement\n2. street sweeping and curbside leaf pick-up\n3. snow clearing operations.\n\nThere is one drawback of this restriction. Residents of Pelham who\nlive in older, 1920's and 1930's era homes, which have smaller,\nrestrictive, tightly walled-in driveways, at all times must park in\ntheir driveways in such a way that their modern cars, built much\nlarger than 1920's and 1930's cars, cannot fit into the driveway and\noften overhang the sidewalk.\n\nDuring the day, the Village requires them to park in the street, but\nthey are without options on where to park, due to the village-wide\nprohibition on overnight parking.\n\nIf these residents attempt to park in their driveways, they often\ncannot exit their cars because of narrow walls on both sides of the\ndriveway, and other similar constrictions. These residents are caught\nbetween a rock wall and a car door. The Village has identified\napproximately 50-60 homes with this kind of situation.\n\n\n\nThe Village of Pelham is requesting this legislation in order to\nprovide relief to residents whose homes have severely restricted\ndriveways.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008 - S8501 - Referred to Rules\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately upon its enactment by the state of New York.\n",
